<main>
<div class="case-hero"><div class="wrap">
  <span class="kind">Software case study<span class="state">, version 0.6.0 released</span></span>
  <h1>MONITROPE</h1>
  <p class="summary">A network monitoring appliance that discovers devices, learns what normal looks like for each one, and explains outages by tracing the probable cause.</p>
  <dl class="facts"><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Self-hosted monitoring appliance</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>v0.6.0 released, October 2026</dd></div><div><dt>Stack</dt><dd>Python, FastAPI, React, Docker</dd></div><div><dt>Install</dt><dd>Own OS installer, or Docker Compose</dd></div></dl>
  
</div></div>
<section><div class="wrap"><h2>Why I Built It</h2><div class="prose"><p>Most monitoring tells you that something is down. I wanted a tool that also says <strong>why</strong>, the way I troubleshoot on the plant floor: understand normal behavior first, then trace a failure back through what depends on what. MONITROPE is that idea turned into software.</p></div></div></section><section><div class="wrap"><h2>What It Does</h2><ul class="steps"><li><div><h3>Discovers and Identifies Devices</h3><p>Builds an inventory of what is on the network and maps how devices link together.</p></div></li><li><div><h3>Learns Normal Behavior</h3><p>Watches each device over a learning period and builds a baseline of what normal looks like.</p></div></li><li><div><h3>Detects and Explains Problems</h3><p>Flags behavior that departs from the baseline and traces outages to a probable cause instead of reporting every downstream symptom.</p></div></li><li><div><h3>Notifies and Records</h3><p>Sends alerts by push, email, webhook or browser, keeps history for replaying incidents, and produces a report of the evidence behind its conclusions.</p></div></li><li><div><h3>Answers Questions</h3><p>Lets you ask about your network in plain language.</p></div></li></ul><p class="caption">Implementation and validation status vary by feature; this page describes the design as released.</p></div></section><section><div class="wrap"><h2>Architecture</h2><div class="diagram"><div class="blocks"><div class="group">Interface</div><div><b>Web app</b><small>React and TypeScript</small></div><div><b>Updates page</b><small>One-button signed updates</small></div><div class="group">Services, in Docker Compose</div><div><b>API</b><small>FastAPI</small></div><div><b>Worker</b><small>Background jobs</small></div><div><b>Monitoring</b><small>Polling and telemetry</small></div><div><b>Learning</b><small>Behavioral baselines</small></div><div><b>Correlation</b><small>Related events</small></div><div><b>Root-cause analysis</b><small>Probable cause</small></div><div><b>Remediation</b><small>Suggested actions</small></div><div class="group">Platform</div><div><b>Database</b><small>SQLAlchemy with Alembic migrations</small></div><div><b>MONITROPE OS</b><small>Debian-based installer image</small></div></div></div><p class="caption">Each service runs as its own container; the OS installer sets the whole stack up on a dedicated machine or virtual machine.</p></div></section><section><div class="wrap"><h2>Releases You Can Trust</h2><div class="diagram"><ol class="chain"><li class=""><b>Build</b><small>CI builds the images and OS installer</small></li><li class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</li><li class=""><b>Sign</b><small>Release manifest signed with a private Ed25519 key</small></li><li class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</li><li class=""><b>Publish</b><small>Attached to a GitHub release</small></li><li class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</li><li class=""><b>Verify</b><small>Appliance checks the signature, SHA-256 and size</small></li><li class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</li><li class=""><b>Install</b><small>Update applied, with rollback if it fails</small></li></ol></div><p class="caption">An appliance refuses any update whose signature or checksums don't match.</p></div></section><section><div class="wrap"><h2>Results so Far</h2><div class="results"><div class="result"><span class="figure">0.6.0</span><p>Released with an installable OS image and signed one-button updates.</p></div><div class="result"><span class="figure">305</span><p>Automated backend tests passing at release.</p></div><div class="result"><span class="figure">8</span><p>Containerized services in the appliance.</p></div></div></div></section><section><div class="wrap"><h2>What&rsquo;s Next</h2><div class="prose"><ul><li>Install on dedicated lab hardware under Proxmox and run it against the PROTO lab.</li><li>Validate discovery, learning and root-cause analysis against real lab incidents, and publish the results here.</li><li>Use it to watch the OT learning environment as it comes online.</li></ul></div></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><h2>Skills Used</h2><ul class="chips"><li>Python</li><li>FastAPI</li><li>SQLAlchemy and Alembic</li><li>React and TypeScript</li><li>Docker Compose</li><li>Debian packaging</li><li>CI/CD with GitHub Actions</li><li>Ed25519 code signing</li><li>Network discovery</li><li>Anomaly detection</li></ul></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><h2>Documentation</h2><ul class="docs"><li><b>Architecture</b><span>The service and platform layout above.</span></li><li><b>Release process</b><span>Build, sign, publish, verify and install, as shown above.</span></li><li><b>Source code</b><span>Kept in a private repository while the project matures; walkthroughs available on request.</span></li><li><b>Development notes</b><span><a href="aiio.html">AIIO Station</a>, the local AI platform that supports MONITROPE development</span></li></ul></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><p class="note"><b>Sanitized for public use.</b> No real addresses, hostnames, credentials or private repository details are shown. Where a feature is still being validated, it is marked as such.</p></div></section>
